/**
 * # Chemical knowledge
 *
 * Chemistry addresses universal physical composition and transformations through
 * the PhysicalPhenomena subject-matter domain. Earth sphere applications are
 * specializations, not an intrinsic boundary of chemical knowledge.
 */
ontology chemistry
	version 1.0
	using imod, physical
	in domain imod:Chemistry;

abstract identity ChemicalIdentity
	"Any chemical, element, compounds, or compound family, but not mixtures."
	requires authority CHEM {
        urn: "klab.authority.pubchem",
        searchLimit: 10,
        gifDepictions: true
    }
;

/**
 * ## Mixture forms
 *
 * Mixture groups Solution, Suspension and Colloid as chemical mixture identities,
 * distinct from the substance and compound identities selected through CHEM.
 * The descriptions follow IUPAC terminology for
 * [solution](https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.S05746),
 * [suspension](https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.S06198), and
 * [colloidal dispersion](https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.C01174).
 */
abstract identity Mixture
	"Any material system made up of two or more different substances 
	 which are mixed but are not combined chemically. A mixture refers 
	 to the physical combination of two or more substances in which the 
	 identities are retained and are mixed in the form of solutions, 
	 suspensions, and colloids."
	 has children 
	 	(Solution 
	 		"A single liquid or solid phase containing multiple substances distinguished as solvent and solutes."
	 	),
	 	(Suspension
	 		"A mixture with solid particles dispersed throughout a liquid medium."
	 	),
	 	(Colloid 
	 		"A mixture whose colloidal-sized dispersed particles or domains differ in composition or state from the continuous medium."
	 	);

/**
 * ## Chemical conversion and solution formation
 *
 * ChemicalReaction describes the conversion of chemical species. Oxidation
 * and Reduction distinguish participants undergoing net electron loss or gain,
 * expressed as an increase or decrease in oxidation number. Electron accounting
 * need not imply a separately observed transfer of free electrons, and oxidation
 * need not involve oxygen. Both descriptions can apply to a redox occurrence
 * from the perspectives of its different participants.
 *
 * The material involved, its chemical classification and the reaction occurrence
 * are distinct observational targets. Observations establish the participant
 * and material context at the stated scope.
 *
 * Dissolution describes material entering a homogeneous solution phase.
 * Dispersed particles in a suspension remain distinct from dissolved material.
 * Solution formation and species conversion may accompany one another, but
 * observing dissolution alone does not establish a chemical reaction. Observing
 * mixing alone does not establish that a solution has formed.
 *
 * Observations identify the participating material, composition and interval.
 * Models describe reaction paths, rates and transfers at that observational
 * scope. A named process does not prescribe a complete reaction mechanism or
 * require the whole observed sample to have reacted or dissolved.
 *
 * The distinctions follow IUPAC terminology for
 * [chemical reaction](https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.C01033),
 * [oxidation](https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.O04362),
 * [reduction](https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.R05222), and
 * [dissolution](https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.D01806).
 */
process ChemicalReaction
    "Interconversion of chemical species.";

process Oxidation
    "A chemical reaction involving net electron loss by a participant, expressed as an increase in oxidation number."
    is chemistry:ChemicalReaction;

process Reduction
    "A chemical reaction involving net electron gain by a participant, expressed as a decrease in oxidation number."
    is chemistry:ChemicalReaction;

process Dissolution
    "Formation of a homogeneous solution phase through the mixing of initially distinct phases.";
